## **What Exactly is a Datafile?**

A datafile is a **physical OS file** (like **`app_data01.dbf`** on Linux or **`app_data01.mdf`** on SQL Server) that stores the actual data of the database — table rows, index entries, undo records, temporary sort results, everything.

Key facts:

- Every datafile belongs to **exactly one tablespace**
- One tablespace can have **many datafiles**
- The datafile is the bridge between Oracle's logical world and the OS's physical world
- Oracle reads and writes datafiles through the **Database Writer (DBWn)** background process — not directly from user processes

When you query a table, Oracle doesn't read directly from the datafile every time. It first checks the **Buffer Cache** in memory. If the data isn't there, it reads from the datafile into the buffer cache, then serves it to you. Writes work similarly — changes go to the buffer cache first, and DBWn flushes them to the datafile later.

---

## **Datafile Internal Structure**

A datafile is not just a raw dump of rows. It has a well-defined internal structure.

### **File Header**

Every datafile begins with a **header block**. This header contains:

- **File number** — Oracle's internal ID for this file
- **Tablespace name** it belongs to
- **Database ID (DBID)** — ensures the file belongs to this database, not another
- **Checkpoint SCN** — the System Change Number at the last checkpoint (explained below)
- **Creation timestamp**
- **Resetlogs SCN** — used during recovery to verify consistency

The file header is critical. If it gets corrupted, Oracle cannot use that datafile at all. The control file also tracks information about each datafile, and Oracle cross-checks both during startup.

### **Oracle Blocks**

After the header, the rest of the datafile is divided into **Oracle blocks** (also called database blocks or data blocks). These are the smallest unit of I/O in Oracle.

The block size is set when the tablespace is created and cannot be changed afterward. Common sizes:

- **8KB** — default, most common
- **4KB, 16KB, 32KB** — also supported

The OS also has a block size (usually 512 bytes to 4KB). Oracle's block size should be a **multiple** of the OS block size. This ensures that one Oracle block read = one or a few clean OS block reads with no partial reads.

---

## **The Relationship Between Datafiles, Control Files, and Redo Logs**

These three types of files together make up the **database**. They work as a team:

text

```
Control File
    - Knows about every datafile (name, size, status, checkpoint SCN)
    - Knows about every redo log file
    - Tracks the overall database checkpoint

Datafiles
    - Store the actual data
    - Have a header with their own checkpoint SCN

Redo Log Files
    - Record every change made to the database
    - Used to replay changes during recovery

```

When Oracle starts up, it compares the checkpoint SCN in the control file against the checkpoint SCN in each datafile header. If they match — the database is consistent and can open normally. If they don't match — Oracle knows that the datafile is behind and needs redo to be applied. This is the foundation of crash recovery.

---

## **Checkpoints — Very Important Concept**

A **checkpoint** is an event where Oracle:

1. Writes all **dirty buffers** (modified data in memory not yet on disk) from the Buffer Cache to the datafiles
1. Updates the datafile headers with the current **SCN**
1. Updates the control file

### **Why Checkpoints Exist**

Imagine Oracle crashes. When it restarts, it needs to know: "what data was safely written to disk and what wasn't?"

The checkpoint SCN in the datafile header answers this. Oracle knows:

- Everything up to this SCN is safely on disk
- Everything after this SCN may be missing from the datafile
- So replay redo logs from this SCN forward to recover the missing changes

Without checkpoints, Oracle would have to replay redo from the very beginning of time every time it restarted — which would be impossibly slow.

### **Types of Checkpoints**

**Full Checkpoint:**\
Writes ALL dirty buffers to disk. Updates all datafile headers and the control file. Happens during:

- **`SHUTDOWN NORMAL / IMMEDIATE`**
- **`ALTER SYSTEM CHECKPOINT`**
- Taking a tablespace offline normally

sql

```
-- Force a manual checkpoint
ALTER SYSTEM CHECKPOINT;

```

**Incremental Checkpoint (Continuous):**\
Oracle doesn't wait for a full checkpoint event. It continuously writes dirty buffers in the background. The **checkpoint position** moves forward constantly. This reduces the amount of redo that needs to be applied in a crash recovery because dirty buffers are being flushed regularly.

**Tablespace Checkpoint:**\
When you take a specific tablespace offline normally, Oracle checkpoints only the datafiles of that tablespace.

**File Checkpoint:**\
When a datafile is taken offline or a backup begins, Oracle checkpoints that specific file.

### **CHECKPOINT vs DBWn**

People confuse these:

- **DBWn (Database Writer)** — the background process that physically writes dirty blocks to datafiles
- **CKPT (Checkpoint process)** — updates the datafile headers and control file with the checkpoint SCN

DBWn does the heavy lifting (disk writes). CKPT just updates the bookkeeping. They work together during a checkpoint event.

---

## **Datafile States**

Oracle tracks the state of each datafile. These states matter for recovery and operations.

{% table %}
- **State**
- **Meaning**
---
- **`ONLINE`**
- Normal, accessible
---
- **`OFFLINE`**
- Taken offline by DBA, not accessible
---
- **`OFFLINE DROP`**
- Datafile was dropped (only in NOARCHIVELOG mode)
---
- **`RECOVER`**
- Needs media recovery before it can come online
---
- **`SYSOFF`**
- System tablespace file taken offline — unusual/emergency
{% /table %}

sql

```
-- Check datafile status
SELECT file#, name, status, checkpoint_change#
FROM v$datafile;

-- Also useful
SELECT file_name, tablespace_name, status, online_status
FROM dba_data_files;

```

---

## **Taking a Datafile Offline and Online**

You can offline/online at the datafile level (more granular than tablespace level):

sql

```
-- Take a specific datafile offline
ALTER DATABASE DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf' OFFLINE;

-- Bring it back (may need recovery first)
ALTER DATABASE DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf' ONLINE;

```

If you take a datafile offline without a normal checkpoint (e.g., the disk is dead), you need media recovery before bringing it back online:

sql

```
-- Recover the datafile (applies archived redo logs)
RECOVER DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf';

-- Then bring online
ALTER DATABASE DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf' ONLINE;

```

This is different from tablespace offline because:

- You can offline a single datafile within a tablespace — the rest of the tablespace still works (as long as the objects don't span that specific file)
- Useful when one disk in a multi-disk tablespace fails

---

## **ARCHIVELOG vs NOARCHIVELOG Mode**

This is one of the most important concepts in database administration and directly affects how datafiles are protected.

### **Redo Log Files — Quick Background**

Every change to every datafile is first recorded in **redo log files**. These are circular — Oracle writes to log 1, fills it, switches to log 2, fills it, switches to log 3, and then back to log 1 (overwriting it).

### **NOARCHIVELOG Mode**

When Oracle switches from a full redo log to the next one, it simply overwrites the old one. The old redo is **gone forever**.

- **Consequence:** You can only recover from a crash if ALL datafiles are consistent. You cannot recover from a disk failure that destroyed a datafile — the redo needed to reconstruct it has been overwritten.
- **Backup strategy:** You must do cold backups (shut down the database, copy all files). Online backups are not possible.
- **Use case:** Development/test databases where data loss is acceptable.

### **ARCHIVELOG Mode**

Before Oracle overwrites a redo log, it copies it to an **archive log destination** (another directory or tape). These archived logs are kept indefinitely.

- **Consequence:** You can recover from any point in time. Lost a datafile? Restore the backup copy and apply all archived logs up to the current moment. You've lost nothing (or almost nothing).
- **Backup strategy:** Hot backups (online backups while database is running) are possible.
- **Use case:** Any production database. Mandatory for serious use.

sql

```
-- Check current mode
SELECT log_mode FROM v$database;

-- Switch to ARCHIVELOG mode (requires restart)
SHUTDOWN IMMEDIATE;
STARTUP MOUNT;
ALTER DATABASE ARCHIVELOG;
ALTER DATABASE OPEN;

-- Verify
ARCHIVE LOG LIST;

```

---

## **Datafile Creation — What Actually Happens**

When you run:

sql

```
CREATE TABLESPACE app_data
DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf' SIZE 100M;

```

Here's what Oracle does step by step:

1. Oracle asks the OS to create a file of 100MB at that path
1. Oracle writes the **file header** to the first block of the file — recording the file number, tablespace name, DBID, creation SCN
1. Oracle formats some blocks as **bitmap blocks** (for locally managed tablespaces) to track free extents
1. Oracle updates the **control file** to record this new datafile — its name, size, tablespace, and current SCN
1. Oracle updates the **data dictionary** (**`dba_data_files`**, **`v$datafile`**, etc.)
1. The tablespace is now ready to use

This entire process is logged in the redo logs so it can be recovered if something crashes mid-creation.

---

## **Datafile Size Limits**

There are limits to how large a datafile can be, based on the Oracle block size:

{% table %}
- **Block Size**
- **Max Datafile Size**
---
- 2KB
- ~8 GB
---
- 4KB
- ~16 GB
---
- 8KB
- ~32 GB
---
- 16KB
- ~64 GB
---
- 32KB
- ~128 GB
{% /table %}

This limit exists because Oracle uses a **block address** that includes a file number and block number. The number of bits available for the block number limits the total addressable blocks per file.

If you need a tablespace larger than the limit, you simply add more datafiles. There's no practical limit to the number of datafiles a tablespace can have (well, Oracle has a maximum of 65,533 datafiles per database total, but you'll never hit that).

**Bigfile Tablespaces** (Oracle 10g+) solve this differently:

sql

```
CREATE BIGFILE TABLESPACE big_data
DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/big_data01.dbf' SIZE 100G;

```

A bigfile tablespace has exactly **one** datafile but that file can be up to **128TB** (with 32KB blocks). Simpler to manage at large scale.

---

## **Temporary Datafiles (Tempfiles)**

Temporary tablespaces use **tempfiles** instead of datafiles. They look similar but behave differently:

- Tempfiles are **not** recovered after a crash — they're just recreated empty
- Tempfiles do **not** generate redo log entries (writes to temp are not logged)
- Tempfiles do **not** appear in **`dba_data_files`** — they appear in **`dba_temp_files`**

sql

```
-- View tempfiles
SELECT file_name, tablespace_name, bytes/1024/1024 AS size_mb
FROM dba_temp_files;

-- Add a tempfile
ALTER TABLESPACE temp ADD TEMPFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/temp02.dbf'
SIZE 100M AUTOEXTEND ON;

```

If a tempfile goes missing (e.g., you accidentally delete it), the database will still open. But any query that needs temp space will fail. You just recreate the tempfile:

sql

```
ALTER TABLESPACE temp ADD TEMPFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/temp01.dbf' SIZE 100M;

```

This is much less serious than a regular datafile going missing.

---

## **Tracking and Monitoring Datafiles**

### **Key Dynamic Performance Views**

sql

```
-- v$datafile: Current state of all datafiles (from control file)
SELECT file#, name, status, bytes/1024/1024 AS size_mb,
       checkpoint_change#, last_change#
FROM v$datafile;

-- v$datafile_header: Info from the datafile headers themselves
SELECT file#, status, checkpoint_change#, to_char(checkpoint_time,'DD-MON-YYYY HH24:MI:SS') AS ckpt_time
FROM v$datafile_header;

-- Compare v$datafile vs v$datafile_header
-- If checkpoint_change# doesn't match → file needs recovery

-- dba_data_files: Data dictionary view (user-friendly)
SELECT file_name, tablespace_name, bytes/1024/1024 AS size_mb,
       maxbytes/1024/1024 AS max_mb, autoextensible, status
FROM dba_data_files
ORDER BY tablespace_name;

-- Find files that need recovery
SELECT * FROM v$recover_file;

-- I/O statistics per datafile (for performance tuning)
SELECT name, phyrds, phywrts, readtim, writetim
FROM v$datafile df JOIN v$filestat fs ON df.file# = fs.file#
ORDER BY phyrds DESC;

```

### **Detecting I/O Hotspots**

The **`v$filestat`** view is very useful for performance. If one datafile has dramatically more reads/writes than others, it's an I/O hotspot. Solutions:

- Move that datafile to a faster disk (SSD)
- Split the tablespace — add another datafile on a different disk so I/O is distributed
- Check if the objects in that tablespace can be reorganized or cached

---

## **What Happens When a Datafile Goes Missing**

Say a disk fails and **`/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf`** is gone. Here's what happens:

1. **If the database is running:** The first time Oracle tries to read or write that file, DBWn gets an I/O error. Oracle marks the file as offline and raises an error. If it's a critical file (SYSTEM tablespace), Oracle may shut down.
1. **If the database is starting up:** Oracle opens the control file, sees the datafile listed, tries to verify the file header, fails, and refuses to open.

**Recovery path (ARCHIVELOG mode):**

sql

```
-- Start in mount state
STARTUP MOUNT;

-- Restore the datafile from backup (OS level or RMAN)
-- Then recover it
RECOVER DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf';

-- Oracle applies archived logs, asks for each one needed
-- When done:
ALTER DATABASE DATAFILE '/u01/oradata/mydb/app_data01.dbf' ONLINE;

-- Open database
ALTER DATABASE OPEN;

```

**Without ARCHIVELOG mode:** You can only restore the entire database from the last cold backup. All changes since that backup are lost.

---

## **Multiplexing Control Files (Related Best Practice)**

The control file records all datafile information. If the control file is lost, Oracle can't find its datafiles. Best practice:

sql

```
-- Check current control files
SELECT name FROM v$controlfile;

-- Add more copies in init.ora / spfile
-- control_files = '/u01/control01.ctl','/u02/control02.ctl','/u03/control03.ctl'

```

Keep control files on **different disks**. If one disk dies, the others keep the database running.

---

## **Key Exam Points**

- A datafile is a physical OS file. It belongs to exactly one tablespace. One tablespace can have many datafiles.
- Every datafile has a **header** containing the file number, DBID, and checkpoint SCN.
- **Checkpoints** write dirty buffer cache blocks to datafiles and update the SCN in the file header — this is how crash recovery knows where to start replaying redo.
- DBWn writes data to datafiles. CKPT updates the file headers. They work together during checkpoints.
- In **ARCHIVELOG mode**, filled redo logs are archived before being overwritten — this enables point-in-time recovery. Production databases must use ARCHIVELOG.
- In **NOARCHIVELOG mode**, old redo is lost — only crash recovery is possible, not media recovery.
- Tempfiles don't generate redo and don't need recovery — losing one is not a crisis.
- **`v$datafile`** reads from the control file. **`v$datafile_header`** reads from the actual file header. Comparing checkpoint SCNs between these two tells you if a file needs recovery.
- Bigfile tablespaces have one giant datafile; regular tablespaces can have many smaller ones.
